<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kublai Khan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1215">1215</a>–<a href="/years/1294">1294</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borjigin">Borjigin</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kublai Khan was a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> who became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-khan">Great Khan</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a> and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan Dynasty</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. He completed the conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty</a> in 1279 and ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khanbaliq">Khanbaliq</a>. His disputed succession contributed to the fragmentation of the Mongol Empire, and his expeditions overseas, including against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, influenced the rise of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kublai-khan.jpg" alt="Kublai Khan" width="320" height="399" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Araniko · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:YuanEmperorAlbumKhubilaiPortrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Kublai was the second son of Tolui and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorghaghtani-beki">Sorghaghtani Beki</a>, and a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>. In 1224, near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ili-river">Ili River</a>, Genghis Khan performed a hunting ceremony on the nine-year-old Kublai and his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongke">Möngke</a>, and is reported to have said that the boy's words were full of wisdom. Kublai was twelve when Genghis died in 1227.</p><p>After his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongke">Möngke</a> became Khan in 1251, Kublai received the viceroyalty over northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and moved his camp to central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inner-mongolia">Inner Mongolia</a>. He is said to have managed his territory well, raising the agricultural output of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a> and increasing spending on social welfare, which won him the respect of Han warlords and helped prepare the founding of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Learning that his younger brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ariq-boke">Ariq Böke</a> had been raising troops, Kublai returned north to find that an assembly at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karakorum">Karakorum</a> had named Ariq Böke Great Khan. Kublai summoned his own assembly, which proclaimed him Great Khan on 15 April 1260 with the support of most senior princes in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toluid-civil-war">Toluid Civil War</a> that followed lasted until 1264 and began the division of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a>. His real power was limited to the Yuan realm.</p><p>Within a decade Kublai saw that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> was his main base. He adopted Chinese political and cultural models, worked to reduce the influence of regional lords, and relied on advisers such as Liu Bingzhong and Xu Heng. He proclaimed the dynastic name Great Yuan in 1271, completed the conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a> by 1279, and became the first non-Han emperor to rule all of China proper. His capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khanbaliq">Khanbaliq</a>, also called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dadu">Dadu</a>, was designed by the architect Ikhtiyar al-Din.</p><p>He made Goryeo a tributary state in 1260, and ordered invasions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>. The attempt on Japan in 1274 used a fleet of 900 ships, and both attempts failed, partly from bad weather or poorly designed ships. These costly campaigns and the issue of paper currency caused <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In his last years Kublai sent his grandson Gammala to Burkhan Khaldun in 1291 to secure his claim to the sacred burial ground of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>. He died on 18 February 1294. His seizure of power in 1260 pushed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a> in a new direction and hastened its disunity, but his willingness to present the Mongol realm as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> brought it international attention. Yuan rule of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> from modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> set a precedent for the later expansion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inner-asia">Inner Asia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kublai Khan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>That changed when Alghu (r. 1260–1266) used the civil war between <b>Kublai</b> and Ariq Böke to assert real independence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chagatai-empire">Chagatai Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Chinese histories of the 7th century counted it the chief tributary state of the South, on a par with Koguryŏ and Baekje, and <b>Kublai Khan</b> ordered a mobile secretariat set up there to dominate the trade of the South China Sea and the Indian Ocean.</blockquote><a href="/empire/champa">Champa · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The shogunate's greatest test came from the Mongols under <b>Kublai Khan</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kamakura-shogunate">Kamakura shogunate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Majapahit's founder Raden Wijaya used a Mongol army sent by <b>Kublai Khan</b> to defeat his rival, then turned on the invaders and drove them from Java.</blockquote><a href="/empire/majapahit-empire">Majapahit Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>After the succession war between <b>Kublai</b> and Ariq Böke (1260–1264), the Golden Horde, Ilkhanate, Chagatai Khanate and Yuan dynasty operated as largely independent states, though they continued to acknowledge a shared Chinggisid heritage.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>That changed when the Yuan dynasty of <b>Kublai Khan</b> demanded submission in the 1270s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pagan-kingdom">Pagan Kingdom · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a>. Died in Khanbaliq, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.93,116.4&amp;year=1266" aria-label="Khanbaliq on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7417 735 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Kublai Khan"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7867 1005h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7867 1005h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=39.93,116.4&amp;year=1266">What was here in 1266? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I worship and pay respect to all four, and pray that he among them who is greatest in heaven in very truth may aid me.&#8221;</p><footer>On the festivals of the Christians, Saracens, Jews and Buddhists (Marco Polo, Travels, book 2, Ramusio's text) &#183; tr. Henry Yule, 1871 (3rd ed., rev. Henri Cordier, 1903) &#183; Marco Polo's account; &quot;all four&quot; are the prophets of those faiths: Jesus, Muhammad, Moses and the Buddha &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/10636/pg10636-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…and thus in the end there will be more Christians here than exist in your part of the world!&#8221;</p><footer>To the Polo brothers, asking the Pope for a hundred learned men (Marco Polo, Travels, book 2, Ramusio's text) &#183; tr. Henry Yule, 1871 (3rd ed., rev. Henri Cordier, 1903) &#183; Marco Polo's account &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/10636/pg10636-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
